Text from Article: I believe that a ‘pivotal act’ is Yudkowsky’s euphemism for acts of terrorism that he believes anyone making AGI must do under certain circumstances to prevent competition and therefore the end of the world, and that many members of this enterprise will understand some offshoot of this idea and will understand such acts to be necessary in the course of pursuing their goals.
Claim: acts of terrorism that he believes anyone making AGI must do

## Your earlier research
The claim mischaracterizes Yudkowsky's concept. In his writing (e.g., the Arbital page on 'pivotal act' and the LessWrong post 'AGI Ruin: A List of Lethalities', https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/uMQ3cqWDPHhjtiesc/agi-ruin-a-list-of-lethalities), a pivotal act is defined as an action by an aligned AGI-wielding actor that substantially reduces the risk of world-ending outcomes, for example by preventing other actors from building unaligned AGI. His standard example is using an AGI to 'burn all GPUs' (destroy rival AI compute). That is a unilateral, possibly illegal or coercive act, and critics have argued it resembles sabotage or terrorism. But Yudkowsky does not call it terrorism, and he doesn't say everyone making AGI 'must' do it. He presents it as something that may be necessary because he judges alignment to be very hard and competition dangerous. He has also said that he prefers non-violent options, and that pivotal acts need not be violent. For example, he has pointed to the possibility of an AGI-enabled act that is not a military attack. He also said he opposes violence as a way to stop AI development. In his TIME op-ed he called for international treaties enforced by state action, not terrorism (https://time.com/6266923/ai-eliminate-human-race-yudkowsky/). The statement that 'pivotal act' is a euphemism for terrorism that anyone making AGI must carry out is therefore an opinionated, loaded characterization. It overstates his position, since it presents an interpretation as fact and treats the act as an obligation on all AGI developers.

