Claim: Effective Altruism and its AI safety efforts are directly beholden to Eliezer Yudkowsky rather than separate from him.

## Your earlier research
The claim is overstated and misleading. Yudkowsky is an influential founder of the rationalist community (LessWrong) and of MIRI, and he shaped early AI-risk thinking. The rationalist and EA communities do overlap, especially in the Bay Area. But "directly beholden" is not supported. Effective Altruism grew out of Oxford philosophy (Toby Ord, Will MacAskill) and GiveWell/Open Philanthropy. Its institutions are separate from Yudkowsky: 80,000 Hours, the Centre for Effective Altruism, and Open Philanthropy. AI safety work in EA is also funded and carried out through other organizations (Anthropic-linked researchers, Redwood, ARC, CAIS, academic labs), and many of them disagree with Yudkowsky. His views are at the extreme end: he favors a halt on AI development and has a very high estimate of doom. Most EA AI safety researchers hold lower p(doom) estimates and pursue different approaches, such as alignment and interpretability. Open Philanthropy has funded MIRI, but it is only one grantee among many. So there is influence and overlap, but not direct control or dependence. No source was searched; this assessment is based on established background knowledge. A general reference is https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effective_altruism.

